ID: Ch3-Intro
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1) {

"So, Barry, let me get this straight," Daren says, leaning forward across the fire. "You're saying that trinket you carry is actually a device that amplifies your physical attributes, powered by ambient magic in the air?"

"Right," I say.

"And you plan to use this thing to go toe-to-toe with the mages in this tournament?"

"Correct."

Daren, Kate, and I sit in a rough ring on the stone floor of a shallow limestone cave we found tucked beneath the northern ridges. The march up from the lowlands carried a steady stench of smoke and decay—half a dozen bodies left cooling in the brush along the trail, stripped of gear and forgotten. Daren has shed his plate armor for a loose woolen tunic, rubbing the red pressure marks where the cuirass bit into his collarbones.

"Kate, I think this afternoon broke the poor boy's mind. He is completely delirious. Would you mind knocking some sense into our companion?"

"No," Kate says, without looking up.

"Because you can't be bothered to—"

"Because he's telling the truth. That device is a stat booster. Most of the channels on it are designed to push physical attributes beyond human limits."

Daren stares at her, his brow furrowing over the crossed scars on his forehead. "Wait. Weren't you the one telling me not to trust him two hours ago? Why are you taking his side now?"

"I'm not taking anyone's side. I've known what those devices do for years. I don't need to trust him to know what's in his hand."

"Fine. I yield." Daren throws his hands up, chuckling under his breath. "You win, Barry. Show us what these stats of yours actually look like."

}
#if(v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1) {

"So, Barry, let me get this straight," Daren says, leaning forward across the fire. "You're saying that trinket you carry is actually a device that amplifies your physical attributes, powered by ambient magic in the air?"

"Right," I say.

"And this device fed you an ancient dialect that let you hold a conversation with two deer?"

"Correct."

Daren, Kate, and I sit on the dry stone floor of our hosts' cavern, the air smelling faintly of sweet lichen and dried mountain grass. The trek up the slopes was grim enough—scattered corpses in the ravines, mages already cutting each other down for standing. Daren has stripped out of his heavy plate and sits in a loose woolen tunic, rolling his broad shoulders to work out the stiffness.

"Barry, there is no such thing as holding a conversation with wild animals. I heard you barking and clicking at them. Pure gibberish. Getting lucky because they guided us to their den doesn't make you a linguist. Kate, could you please knock some sense into our companion?"

"No," Kate says, without looking up.

"Because you can't be bothered to—"

"Because he's telling the truth. That device is a stat booster, and one of its functions translates ancient tongues directly into the user's mind."

Daren blinks at her, the crossed scars on his forehead shifting with his frown. "Wait. Weren't you telling me not to trust him two hours ago? Why are you taking his side now?"

"I'm not taking anyone's side. I've known what those devices do for years. I don't need to trust him to know what's in his hand."

"Fine. I yield." Daren raises his palms in defeat. "You win, Barry. Show us what these stats of yours actually look like."

}
choice("Continue", Ch3-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch3-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: Ch3-Intro2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1) {
I tilt the stat booster so the dim green glow of its nine channels reflects against Daren's face. He studies the runic columns in silence, stroking his jaw.

"All right. Here is my two copper pieces. If we keep moving together, and if that trinket truly does what you claim, the single greatest asset you could give us is maxing out that observation stat."

"Observation?" I ask. "Out of all nine?"

"Observation detects illusions. The surest sign you've stepped inside a phantasm is a subtle flaw in the landscape. A drift of dry leaves that doesn't crunch, a sun sitting three fingers higher than it was a minute ago, shadows falling the wrong way across the grass. I am terrible at catching those discrepancies. I usually realize I'm trapped when the blade is already at my throat. It hasn't killed me yet, but I've never fought the deadliest mages alive on one continent before. Even if you pump every point into raw muscle, you won't match Kate or me in straight combat, let alone the veterans hunting in these woods. But sharp eyes? Sharp eyes might keep all three of us breathing."

Daren lets the silence settle over the cave.

"That's my counsel, anyway. Take it or leave it. The device is yours to command. But make your choice tonight so we can coordinate our tactics. We cannot walk into tomorrow's skirmish blind."

}
#if(v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1) {
I tilt the stat booster so the dim green glow of its nine channels reflects against Daren's face. He studies the runic columns in silence, stroking his jaw.

"All right. Here is my two copper pieces. If we keep moving together, and if that trinket truly does what you claim, the single greatest asset you could give us is maxing out that observation stat."

"Observation?" I ask. "Out of all nine?"

"Observation detects illusions. The surest sign you've stepped inside a phantasm is a subtle flaw in the landscape. A drift of dry leaves that doesn't crunch, a sun sitting three fingers higher than it was a minute ago, shadows falling the wrong way across the grass. I am terrible at catching those discrepancies. I usually realize I'm trapped when the blade is already at my throat. It hasn't killed me yet, but I've never fought the deadliest mages alive on one continent before. Even if you pump every point into raw muscle, you won't match Kate or me in straight combat, let alone the veterans hunting in these woods. But sharp eyes? Sharp eyes might keep all three of us breathing."

Daren lets the thought hang in the air.

"That's my counsel, anyway. Take it or leave it. The device is yours to command."

"I hope I am not intruding," Elaria says as she glides into the firelight. "You have traveled far. You must require sustenance."

With a gentle nudge of her front hoof, she slides a wide, steaming clay platter toward the center of our circle. Rich, roasted meat glistens under the embers. The aroma is spiced, savory, and unmistakably... venison.

}
choice("Focus on avoiding attacks and baiting opponents", Ch3-Tactics, v_current_scene = Ch3-Tactics, v_ch3_tactics = 1) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1)
choice("Focus on fighting the enemies head on", Ch3-Tactics, v_current_scene = Ch3-Tactics, v_ch3_tactics = 2) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1)
choice("Focus on observing the battlefield and providing tactical information", Ch3-Tactics, v_current_scene = Ch3-Tactics, v_ch3_tactics = 3) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction != 1)
choice("Ask her how she got the plate", Ch3-Plate, v_current_scene = Ch3-Plate) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1)
choice("Ask her if they're cannibals", Ch3-Cannibals, v_current_scene = Ch3-Cannibals) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1)
choice("Call out to see if Molan is still alive", Ch3-Molan, v_current_scene = Ch3-Molan) if (v_ch2_deer_interaction == 1)


ID: Ch3-Plate
TEXT:

"How did you get that plate?" I ask her in the ancient tongue. "Surely you didn't carry ceramicware from the nearest city?"

"No, of course not," she replies with a gentle tilt of her antlers. "I conjured it, just as I conjured the feast before you."

"You can... cast magic?"

Across the fire, Daren leans toward Kate, muttering behind his hand.

"I swear, watching him make those clicking noises is going to give me a migraine."

Elaria ignores him entirely, keeping her dark, luminous eyes on me.

"Many creatures on Varathia possess this gift. It was granted to us by our guardian, the great golden fox. Molan's father was far more adept at the craft than I, but I manage well enough."

choice("Ask about the golden fox", Ch3-Fox, v_current_scene = Ch3-Fox)
choice("Ask her what happened to Molan's father", Ch3-Father, v_current_scene = Ch3-Father)


ID: Ch3-Cannibals
TEXT:

"Wait, so the two of you are cannibals?" I blurt out.

The doe lets out a soft, trilling huff that sounds remarkably like laughter.

"No, outlander. Though I understand your alarm. Perhaps choosing venison was an indelicate gesture. Our protector, the great golden fox, taught us the discipline of food conjuration, and I assumed two-legged hunters preferred roasted meat over wild greens. I conjured the plate as well, knowing humans dislike eating from bare stone."

"You can... cast magic?"

Across the fire, Daren mutters to Kate under his breath.

"I swear, watching him make those clicking noises is going to give me a migraine."

Elaria ignores the murmur and continues smoothly.

"Many beasts across Varathia share this gift. It is our protector's legacy. Molan's father commanded far greater power than I, but I do what I can."

choice("Ask about the golden fox", Ch3-Fox, v_current_scene = Ch3-Fox)
choice("Ask her what happened to Molan's father", Ch3-Father, v_current_scene = Ch3-Father)


ID: Ch3-Molan
TEXT:

A cold knot tightens in my gut. I look down at the roasted meat, then scan the shadows behind Elaria. I haven't seen the fawn since we stepped inside. No... she wouldn't. Would she?

"Molan?" I scramble to my feet, wheeling toward the dark recesses of the cave. "Molan!"

"Over here," Molan pipes up from a mossy hollow near the back wall, his ears twitching lazily.

My breath catches, and I sink back down against the rock. Gods. I am letting this tournament shred my nerves. Elaria watches my sudden panic with wide eyes, then lowers her head as understanding dawns.

"By the great fox... you thought I would harm my own child? Molan is my entire life."

"I'm sorry," I mutter, rubbing the back of my neck. "The killing out there has my head spinning. I didn't mean to insult you."

"No apology is required, outlander. Suspicion is natural in these times. Serving deer meat was perhaps poorly considered, but no living creature died for this meal. The golden fox bestowed the art of conjuration upon us centuries ago. I even formed the platter so you would not have to dine off cold stone."

"You can cast magic?"

"Many of us can. A blessing from our guardian. Molan's father possessed a much stronger grasp of the craft, but I manage what I must."

choice("Ask about the golden fox", Ch3-Fox, v_current_scene = Ch3-Fox)
choice("Ask her what happened to Molan's father", Ch3-Father, v_current_scene = Ch3-Father)


ID: Ch3-Fox
TEXT:

"Who is this golden fox you speak of? Your leader?"

"Our guardian spirit," Elaria says, reverence softening her tone. "An ancient entity that has shepherded the beasts of this land for millennia. She is our teacher, our shield, and the standard by which we live."

Oblivious to our words, Daren stops waiting on my translation and reaches into the platter, pulling free a tender strip of meat. He holds a portion toward Kate.

"Barry is wrapped up in his animal council. Eat while the grease is hot, or it'll congeal."

"Hush. I am trying to listen," Kate snaps.

"You're joking. You understand what they're saying?"

"Only fragments. I need silence to piece the grammar together. Quiet down."

Kate's concentration draws Elaria's attention.

"You need not feel shame for your stumbling grasp of the elder tongue, daughter of the outlanders," the doe speaks gently in the ancient language. "Kate, if I recall your name correctly? Join our circle if you wish. I will speak in simple phrasing to aid your ear."

Kate flinches slightly, caught unprepared. She struggles through the syllables, her accent heavy and stiff.

"I... did not... know that you... understood Common."

"I understand every word, but speech in your tongue is forbidden to us. The golden fox decreed it decades ago, when the sport hunts out of Olmnar began. For centuries before that, beast and man shared this continent in peace. But forty winters past, the nobles of Olmnar began hunting magical beasts for amusement and trophies. The fox severed our kinship and banned the Common tongue from our throats forever."

"So... not allowed... to speak with us?" Kate manages.

"I am forbidden from uttering your words, not from holding council through the elder tongue. I revere the golden fox, yet I carry sorrow for her decree. The divide between our kinds only deepens the bloodshed. Most humans have long forgotten food conjuration, and without our guidance, they hunt flesh out of hunger rather than sport. What began as cruelty in Olmnar has become survival for the desperate. This rift has claimed too many lives—including Molan's father. The weight of it grows heavier each passing year."

choice("Continue", Ch3-Deer-end, v_current_scene = Ch3-Deer-end)


ID: Ch3-Father
TEXT:

"What happened to Molan's father?"

"Hunters from the south cornered us three winters ago," Elaria says softly. "He stood between them and our fawn. He held the pass until we reached the deep caves, but he never followed."

"I'm sorry. I shouldn't have brought it up."

"Do not burden yourself. You could not have known."

Across the fire, Daren gives up on waiting for me and digs into the steaming platter, offering a portion of roasted venison to Kate.

"Barry is having a grand old time with his forest council. Eat while it's warm, Kate, or it'll turn to grease."

"Hush. I am trying to listen," Kate snaps.

"You're joking. You understand what they're saying?"

"Only fragments. I need silence to catch the cadence. Quiet down."

Kate's sharp tone catches Elaria's attention.

"You need not feel shame for your stumbling grasp of the elder tongue, daughter of the outlanders," the doe speaks gently in the ancient language. "Kate, if I recall your name correctly? Join our circle if you wish. I will speak in simple phrasing to aid your ear."

Kate flinches slightly, caught off guard. She struggles through the syllables, her pronunciation stiff and deliberate.

"I... did not... know that you... understood Common."

"I understand every word, but speech in your tongue is forbidden to us. The golden fox decreed it decades ago, when the sport hunts out of Olmnar began. For centuries before that, beast and man shared this continent in peace. But forty winters past, the nobles of Olmnar began hunting magical beasts for amusement and trophies. The fox severed our kinship and banned the Common tongue from our throats forever."

"So... not allowed... to speak with us?" Kate manages.

"I am forbidden from uttering your words, not from holding council through the elder tongue. The great golden fox is an ancient spirit that has guided the beasts of Varathia for thousands of years. She is our shepherd, our teacher, and our shield. I respect her judgment, yet I carry sorrow for the rift it caused. Most humans have long forgotten food conjuration, and without our guidance, they hunt flesh out of hunger rather than sport. What began as cruelty in Olmnar has become survival for the desperate. This rift has claimed too many lives—including Molan's father. The weight of it grows heavier each passing year."

choice("Continue", Ch3-Deer-end, v_current_scene = Ch3-Deer-end)


ID: Ch3-Deer-end
TEXT:

Elaria bows her head, letting the quiet linger.

"Forgive an old doe's rambling. My debt to you stands. Our home is your refuge for as long as you require rest. Eat your fill, and ask without hesitation if you need more. I will be near my son."

With a quiet step, she retreats toward the darker alcove where Molan rests curled upon a bed of dry moss.

Daren wipes grease from his chin, shaking his head with a grin.

"All right," he says. "I have exactly one vital question. Did you ask if there's a second course?"

He taps the edge of the emptied platter.

"She said we can ask for a refill whenever we want," I tell him.

"Magnificent! Then no one needs to hold back."

Once we finish the meal and quench the embers to avoid drawing eyes from the ridges, Daren leans back against the cave wall, returning to business.

"All right, Barry. We need your decision. How are you setting that booster of yours for the days ahead? We have to shape our combat plan around what you can actually do."

choice("Focus on avoiding attacks and baiting opponents", Ch3-Tactics, v_current_scene = Ch3-Tactics, v_ch3_tactics = 1)
choice("Focus on fighting the enemies head on", Ch3-Tactics, v_current_scene = Ch3-Tactics, v_ch3_tactics = 2)
choice("Focus on observing the battlefield and providing tactical information", Ch3-Tactics, v_current_scene = Ch3-Tactics, v_ch3_tactics = 3)


ID: Ch3-Tactics
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_tactics == 1) {
I weigh the options in my head before meeting Daren's eyes.

"I'm planning to focus primarily on avoiding attacks and drawing opponents' attention from now on."

"High speed and sharp reflexes, then," Daren nods, tracing lines on the stone floor. "Premonition and combat technique to back it up. That gives you evasive versatility. Just keep your distance and don't play the hero. Draw their spells, make them waste energy, and let Kate and me land the heavy blows."

Daren turns toward Kate.

"As for our pairing. After that enchanter and his sons yesterday, I'd rather not lock swords with weapon enchanters unless there's no way around it. When we run into them, they're yours."

Kate gives a single, firm nod.

"Elementalists, summoners, and shapeshifters are my domain," Daren continues. "I know how to shut them down."

He is talking about mage specializations. Most practitioners spend their lives refining one specific school rather than spreading their mana thin across generalist casting.

Weapon enchanters weave mana directly into steel and armor, turning mundane blades razor-sharp and virtually unbreakable. Because their magic is tied to physical gear, they almost always train extensively in martial arts.

Elementalists possess an inborn affinity for one of the four prime elements—fire, water, air, or earth. Their raw destructive output is immense, but their reliance on instinct leaves them vulnerable to specialized counter-magic and arcane theory.

Summoners draw creatures across the planes to fight on their behalf. The ritual leaves them stationary during casting, so they surround themselves with defensive wards while their beasts press the attack.

Shapeshifters take the opposite path, molding their own flesh and bone into predatory beasts. It removes the summoner's vulnerability, but prevents them from weaving external spells while shifted.

"What about you, Kate?" Daren asks. "Any particular blind spots we should cover?"

"Yes," she says flatly.

"Care to share them?"

"I do not."

Daren shrugs, unbothered.

"Suit yourself. That's enough strategy for one night. First watch falls to Barry and me. Up you get, Barry."

I follow Daren through the mouth of the cave. We climb the rocky incline above the entrance to reach a wide granite ledge overlooking the valley. The night sky is clear, cold, and crowded with brilliant stars. Nothing moves in the valley below without catching the moonlight.

}
#if(v_ch3_tactics == 2) {
I weigh the options in my head before meeting Daren's eyes.

"I'm planning to fight alongside you two, as crazy as it may sound. Otherwise, I'd just be dragging you down."

Daren lets out a heavy breath, shaking his head with a faint smile.

"I had a feeling you'd say that. Strength, toughness, and combat technique, then. With premonition to keep ambushers from taking your head off. Just don't overestimate what that device can do. Let Kate and me handle the heavy hitters. You keep the skirmishers and foot soldiers off our flanks."

Daren turns toward Kate.

"As for our pairing. After that enchanter and his sons yesterday, I'd rather not lock swords with weapon enchanters unless there's no way around it. When we run into them, they're yours."

