ID: Ch3-Vantage
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch3_message,1) if (v_ancient_languages >= 2 && v_perception >= 1)
set(v_ch3_star_message,0) if (v_perception < 1)
set(v_ch3_star_message,1) if (v_perception >= 1 && v_ancient_languages < 2)
set(v_ch3_star_message,2) if (v_ancient_languages >= 2 && v_perception >= 1)
achievement("A message in the sky",v_ac_ch3_message)
#if(v_perception < 1) {

Up on the mountain ledge, the wind carries a sharp, biting chill. Daren leans against a slab of weathered granite, looking out across the moonlit ridges, and talks about his years on the road. He speaks of fifty-foot giants roaming the frost plains of the Northern Continent, of breaking the dreaded Iknar plague in the east with white-magic purifications, of bringing down a green wyvern in the northern crags with a single enchanted strike.

Standing beside him, I listen to the cadence of his voice and understand, for the first time, why I lunged in front of Cutthroat Dave's blades yesterday. I grew up reading tales of legendary healers in shining plate—heroes who rode into ruined villages and set the world right. Daren is the living embodiment of every boyhood fantasy I ever chased.

"I have crossed half the world, Barry," Daren says, his voice dropping low. "And wherever I set foot, the pattern never breaks. The strong grind the weak into the dirt. The many butcher the few. Starvation, plagues, warring warlords, beasts tearing through farmsteads. I have lived alongside death my entire life, but the sight of it never stops hurting. Seeing those slaughtered mages yesterday... the stench, the empty eyes... it felt like a fist crushing my ribs. As if every dying gasp was echoing inside my own chest. What about you, Barry? What did you feel when you saw all those dead people?"

}
#if(v_perception >= 1 && v_ancient_languages < 2) {

I scan the sky above the peaks. The stars shine with unnatural brilliance tonight, their clusters arranged with deliberate, geometric symmetry—almost forming lines of glyphs across the dark canopy.

I shake my head. The shapes resemble no script I ever encountered in my studies. A trick of the mountain air and starlight, nothing more.

Beside me on the granite ledge, Daren looks out across the moonlit ridges and talks about his years on the road. He speaks of fifty-foot giants roaming the frost plains of the Northern Continent, of breaking the dreaded Iknar plague in the east with white-magic purifications, of bringing down a green wyvern in the northern crags with a single enchanted strike.

Standing beside him, I listen to the cadence of his voice and understand, for the first time, why I lunged in front of Cutthroat Dave's blades yesterday. I grew up reading tales of legendary healers in shining plate—heroes who rode into ruined villages and set the world right. Daren is the living embodiment of every boyhood fantasy I ever chased.

"I have crossed half the world, Barry," Daren says, his voice dropping low. "And wherever I set foot, the pattern never breaks. The strong grind the weak into the dirt. The many butcher the few. Starvation, plagues, warring warlords, beasts tearing through farmsteads. I have lived alongside death my entire life, but the sight of it never stops hurting. Seeing those slaughtered mages yesterday... the stench, the empty eyes... it felt like a fist crushing my ribs. As if every dying gasp was echoing inside my own chest. What about you, Barry? What did you feel when you saw all those dead people?"

}
#if(v_ancient_languages >= 2 && v_perception >= 1) {

I scan the sky above the peaks. The stars burn with unnatural sharpness, their alignment forming unmistakable geometric patterns across the dark dome of the sky.

The ancient language burned into my mind by the booster translates the celestial runes in an instant: "The dragon knows where to find the source of all magic."

A dragon on Varathia? Carrying knowledge of the Magium itself? Star-manipulation spells for long-range messaging exist only in dusty folklore, right alongside ancient dragons. I force my eyes back down toward the valley, filing the phrase away as I return to our watch.

Beside me on the granite ledge, Daren looks out across the moonlit ridges and talks about his years on the road. He speaks of fifty-foot giants roaming the frost plains of the Northern Continent, of breaking the dreaded Iknar plague in the east with white-magic purifications, of bringing down a green wyvern in the northern crags with a single enchanted strike.

Standing beside him, I listen to the cadence of his voice and understand, for the first time, why I lunged in front of Cutthroat Dave's blades yesterday. I grew up reading tales of legendary healers in shining plate—heroes who rode into ruined villages and set the world right. Daren is the living embodiment of every boyhood fantasy I ever chased.

"I have crossed half the world, Barry," Daren says, his voice dropping low. "And wherever I set foot, the pattern never breaks. The strong grind the weak into the dirt. The many butcher the few. Starvation, plagues, warring warlords, beasts tearing through farmsteads. I have lived alongside death my entire life, but the sight of it never stops hurting. Seeing those slaughtered mages yesterday... the stench, the empty eyes... it felt like a fist crushing my ribs. As if every dying gasp was echoing inside my own chest. What about you, Barry? What did you feel when you saw all those dead people?"

}
choice(""I was just busy thinking of how to stay alive at the time."", Ch3-Busy, v_current_scene = Ch3-Busy)
choice(""I felt sorry for them, but I didn't let it affect me."", Ch3-Sorry, v_current_scene = Ch3-Sorry)
choice(""I was almost about to puke."", Ch3-Puke, v_current_scene = Ch3-Puke)


ID: Ch3-Busy
TEXT:

"I didn't really have time to feel anything. I guess I was too preoccupied with staying alive."

"I almost forgot," Daren says, clapping a heavy hand on my shoulder. "That was your first true skirmish, wasn't it? You kept your head when seasoned men panic. Take pride in that, Barry."

He turns back toward the distant treeline, the wind tugging at his cloak.

"When news of this tournament reached the east, I took it as a sign. A lifetime of healing, of mending broken bones and burying plague victims, and yet the suffering never ends. No matter how many I save, the world remains broken. But the Magium... with power on that scale, I might actually change things."

He glances sideways at me, catching the tension in my posture.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Worried, v_current_scene = Ch3-Worried)


ID: Ch3-Sorry
TEXT:

"I felt sorry for them. But I guess I came here prepared for the worst. I knew I'd be seeing a lot of death along my way. So I've decided to not let it affect me."

"Then you carry a harder resolve than I do, Barry."

Daren turns back toward the distant treeline, the wind tugging at his cloak.

"When news of this tournament reached the east, I took it as a sign. A lifetime of healing, of mending broken bones and burying plague victims, and yet the suffering never ends. No matter how many I save, the world remains broken. But the Magium... with power on that scale, I might actually change things."

He glances sideways at me, catching the tension in my posture.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Worried, v_current_scene = Ch3-Worried)


ID: Ch3-Puke
TEXT:

"I was almost about to puke. It was my first time seeing so many dead bodies in one place."

"The sickness passes," Daren says quietly. "Though I fear by the time this week ends, the sight and reek of blood will feel as common as dirt."

He turns back toward the distant treeline, the wind tugging at his cloak.

"When news of this tournament reached the east, I took it as a sign. A lifetime of healing, of mending broken bones and burying plague victims, and yet the suffering never ends. No matter how many I save, the world remains broken. But the Magium... with power on that scale, I might actually change things."

He glances sideways at me, catching the tension in my posture.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Worried, v_current_scene = Ch3-Worried)


ID: Ch3-Worried
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 1) {
"There is no reason the Magium cannot serve us both, Barry. If I win this tournament, I vow to use its power to fulfill your dream. I only ask that you do the same for me if you claim the prize."

The complete absence of deceit in his voice catches me off balance.

"Of course I will," I say. "Assuming we don't already know which one of us is taking first place."

Daren barks a laugh that echoes off the rocks. "Ha! That's the spirit!"

He leans against the stone, letting a companionable silence linger before a smirk tugs at his mouth.

"So. How are things progressing with Kate? Have you made any headway?"

}
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 2) {
"Rest easy, Barry. I promised to get you safely off this continent, and I keep my vows. Even if escorting you costs me ground in the tournament, I will see you on a ship home before I rest."

"I... thanks, Daren."

I swallow hard, mentally reminding myself that I need a plausible excuse to stay before we reach the harbor cities.

Daren leans against the stone, letting the silence settle before a sly smirk tugs at his mouth.

"So. How are things progressing with Kate? Have you made any headway?"

}
#if(v_ch2_human_reason == 3) {
"Rest easy, Barry. I gave you my word that we will find a cure for your sister. Even if searching for it puts me at a disadvantage in the trials, I won't rest until my debt to you is settled."

"I... thanks, Daren."

I suppress a grimace, making a mental note to keep the sick sister story airtight whenever he brings it up.

Daren leans against the stone, letting the silence settle before a sly smirk tugs at his mouth.

"So. How are things progressing with Kate? Have you made any headway?"

}
choice(""I'm not trying to seduce her, Daren."", Ch3-Seduce, v_current_scene = Ch3-Seduce, v_ch3_seduce = 1, v_available_points = +6, v_available_points_aux = +6)
choice(""She still hasn't given in to my charms."", Ch3-Charm, v_current_scene = Ch3-Charm, v_ch3_seduce = 2, v_available_points = +6, v_available_points_aux = +6)
choice(""She's eating out of the palm of my hand!"", Ch3-Palm, v_current_scene = Ch3-Palm, v_ch3_seduce = 3, v_available_points = +6, v_available_points_aux = +6)


ID: Ch3-Seduce
TEXT:

"I'm not trying to seduce her, Daren."

"Of course you aren't," Daren drawls with heavy theatrical sarcasm.

The humor slips from his face, replaced by the grim sobriety of a seasoned crusader.

"Listen to me, Barry. I know your heart is in the right place. But women are not born mages. Magic does not flow through female bloodlines on this world. I cannot explain why her aura feels different to you, or why the banshee inside her remains dormant, but it is a ticking fuse. Eventually the creature awakens, and when it does, I need you ready."

He reaches into a leather pouch at his waist and produces a compact copper-and-crystal token, placing it in my palm. It is a military transceiver, tuned to broadcast short-range pulses without making a sound.

"Keep this on you at all times. If danger closes in, tap the rune three times. I will come running."

"Promise me one thing first," I say, closing my fingers over the device.

"Name it."

"Promise you won't cast a purification spell on her unless you see her transform with your own eyes."

Daren lets out a long, reluctant breath.

"You ask a dangerous favor, Barry. If she turns, every second of hesitation costs lives. But if your conviction runs this deep... I give you my word."

He pushes off the rock.

"Come. Time to wake Kate for second watch."

As we scramble down the stony path toward the cave, the stat booster at my belt emits a sharp, rhythmic chime. I pull it out into the moonlight: six points added to the available pool. A massive magical clash must have concluded somewhere in the valley.

choice("Invest points now", Ch3-Seduce-spent, v_current_scene = Ch3-Seduce-spent, special:stats)
choice("Invest points later(by pressing the stats button on the top of the screen)", Ch3-Kate-debt, v_current_scene = Ch3-Kate-debt)


ID: Ch3-Charm
TEXT:

"She still hasn't given in to my charms."

Daren chuckles, shaking his head.

"Take your time, lad. We have the whole tournament ahead of us."

The humor slips from his face, replaced by the grim sobriety of a seasoned crusader.

"Listen to me, Barry. I know your heart is in the right place. But women are not born mages. Magic does not flow through female bloodlines on this world. I cannot explain why her aura feels different to you, or why the banshee inside her remains dormant, but it is a ticking fuse. Eventually the creature awakens, and when it does, I need you ready."

