choice("Continue", Ch3-Kate-debt, v_current_scene = Ch3-Kate-debt)


ID: Ch3-Kate-debt
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 0) {
I slip the stat booster into my tunic and follow Daren into the cave. We wake Kate, who slips outside into the shadows to take second watch while Daren and I drop onto our bedrolls.

Hours later, a hand grips my shoulder and shakes me awake. Kate leans over me, her voice a harsh whisper barely louder than the draft whistling through the rocks.

"Wake up, stillwater. I need your eyes on something."

I blink into the darkness, sitting up slowly. "You need my help?"

"Quiet. You'll wake Daren. Yes. Come outside and look at a site in the trees. It won't take two minutes."

"And why would I help you? You've done nothing but glare at me and question every breath I take since we met."

"Because then I owe you a debt," she says evenly. "And I never leave a ledger open."

Her expression is dead serious. Whatever lies out in those woods matters to her far more than she lets on.

"Why me instead of Daren? He's the legendary mage here, not me."

"Because Daren knows formal schools and white magic. You know the obscure corners. You understand how a stat booster functions, which almost no one outside Varathia does. And there is a question I intend to ask in private. Move, before Daren stirs."

We slip through the entrance and plunge into the dense pines. The moon hangs midway down the western sky; I cannot have slept for more than three hours.

We walk in silence for several minutes, dry needles crunching under our boots.

}
#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 1 && v_ch2_not_owe_debt == 1) {
I slip the stat booster into my tunic and follow Daren into the cave. We wake Kate, who slips outside into the shadows to take second watch while Daren and I drop onto our bedrolls.

Hours later, a hand grips my shoulder and shakes me awake. Kate leans over me, her voice a harsh whisper barely louder than the draft whistling through the rocks.

"Wake up, stillwater. Time to settle your account."

I rub the sleep from my eyes, remembering our argument after the ambush.

"I thought I made it clear I don't owe you anything."

"You did," she whispers, her gaze sharp in the gloom. "Consider the alternative. Default on your debt tonight, and next time a blade comes at your ribs, I look the other way. Do you want that hanging over you for the rest of the tournament? All I ask is two minutes of your time in the woods. Come look at a site, and your slate is clean."

She isn't bluffing. This matters to her.

"Why me instead of Daren? He's the legendary mage here, not me."

"Because Daren knows formal schools and white magic. You know the obscure corners. You understand how a stat booster functions, which almost no one outside Varathia does. And there is a question I intend to ask in private. Move, before Daren stirs."

We slip through the entrance and plunge into the dense pines. The moon hangs midway down the western sky; I cannot have slept for more than three hours.

We walk in silence for several minutes, dry needles crunching under our boots.

}
#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 1 && v_ch2_not_owe_debt == 0) {
I slip the stat booster into my tunic and follow Daren into the cave. We wake Kate, who slips outside into the shadows to take second watch while Daren and I drop onto our bedrolls.

Hours later, a hand grips my shoulder and shakes me awake. Kate leans over me, her voice a harsh whisper barely louder than the draft whistling through the rocks.

"Wake up, stillwater. Time to settle your account."

I sit up against the stone wall, clearing the grogginess from my throat.

"And how exactly do you expect me to pay it?"

"Walk with me into the woods and examine a site. Two minutes of your time, and we call it even."

She isn't bluffing. This matters to her.

"Why me instead of Daren? He's the legendary mage here, not me."

"Because Daren knows formal schools and white magic. You know the obscure corners. You understand how a stat booster functions, which almost no one outside Varathia does. And there is a question I intend to ask in private. Move, before Daren stirs."

We slip through the entrance and plunge into the dense pines. The moon hangs midway down the western sky; I cannot have slept for more than three hours.

We walk in silence for several minutes, dry needles crunching under our boots.

}
choice("Ask her how come she knows how the stat device works", Ch3-Awkward, v_current_scene = Ch3-Awkward, v_ch3_awkward = 1)
choice("Ask her what she wanted to discuss with me in private", Ch3-Awkward, v_current_scene = Ch3-Awkward, v_ch3_awkward = 2)


ID: Ch3-Awkward
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_awkward == 1) {
"How do you know what stat boosters actually do?" I ask her.

Kate walks with her eyes fixed ahead, silent for a moment as she weighs how much truth to part with.

"Stat boosters were invented on Varathia. I was born on this continent. People from here recognize the hardware. Outsiders rarely do."

"You were born here? I thought you arrived on the tournament ships."

"I was born here, but I didn't remain. Returning to Varathia after leaving is almost impossible by ordinary means. I entered the tournament for passage. I have unfinished business to settle."

"And this site you're leading me to ties into that business?"

She gives no answer, but her jaw tightens. She stops abruptly in a small moonlit clearing and turns to face me squarely.

}
#if(v_ch3_awkward == 2) {
"So what did you want to ask me in private?"

Kate stops abruptly in a small moonlit clearing and turns to face me squarely.

}
choice("Continue", Ch3-Bugging, v_current_scene = Ch3-Bugging)


ID: Ch3-Bugging
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_kate_whisper == 1) {
"There is something that has bothered me since yesterday," she says, her dark eyes searching mine.

"When you and Daren first saw me, you whispered between yourselves for nearly a full minute. You claimed you were debating my looks, and I let it pass. But an entire day went by, and neither of you uttered a single word about banshees. Not even after that enchanter shouted it across the clearing during our battle. You two were debating whether I was a monster, and you decided against purification."

She steps closer, the cold radiance of her aura prickling against my skin.

"Why? I approached Daren hoping his reputation for mercy meant he wouldn't strike me down on sight. But I put my life on the line by stepping into his path. Why did Daren hold back when every scrap of evidence said banshee?"

She still distrusts me. If I admit I was the one who talked Daren down, she will either call me a liar or suspect an ulterior motive and lose whatever faith she has in Daren's protection.

"Daren told me he faced a banshee once before," I lie, keeping my voice steady. "He said your aura felt different, and he chose to wait and watch."

Tension bleeds from her shoulders, though her posture remains rigid.

"I see. That is what I needed to know. Let us keep moving."

"Wait. Why ask me? Why not ask Daren directly?"

"Because Daren wields white magic. Purification is his duty. I had no desire to put the thought into his head if he hadn't considered it yet."

"And why take my word for it?"

"Because you are a terrible liar," she says coldly. "Your face gives away everything you think."

I keep my mouth shut.

}
#if(v_ch2_kate_whisper == 2) {
