choice("Continue", Ch3-Born, v_current_scene = Ch3-Born)


ID: Ch3-Born
TEXT:

"Well, in that case, I have a question of my own. If you aren't a banshee, you can't be a mage either. Women cannot be born with magic. So what are you?"

"Who said I was born a mage?"

The words hang in the night air. My mind stalls, turning the phrase over.

"Wait. You're saying you were born an ordinary human, and became a mage later?"

"That is exactly what I am saying."

"How?" My voice cracks with sudden intensity. "Did you touch the Magium? How did you do it? Tell me!"

The excitement spills through before I can check it. Kate stares at me, bewilderment hardening into sudden, cold realization.

"By the gods," she whispers. "You weren't bluffing. You truly are an ordinary human, and you came to this bloodbath believing you could make yourself a mage."

She shakes her head, her voice turning razor-sharp.

"Listen to me. I don't care what childhood obsession drove you into this suicide pact. The only things waiting for you at the end of that path are agony and despair. Turn around, find a ship at the coast, and you might live to see thirty."

"You talk about agony and despair, but you became a mage. You must understand why I want this."

"I do not. Becoming a mage was never my choice. And I am done discussing it. I gave you my warning. Heed it or die. Not another word."

She wheels around and strides into the dark woods. I hurry after her, the silence between us heavy and strained for the next ten minutes.

As the trees thin and the mountain ridge recedes far behind us, I catch up to her shoulder.

"Wait," I say.

"What now?"

"We are too far from the cave. What if someone attacks Daren while he's asleep?"

"You don't need to worry about Daren."

choice(""Why not?"", Ch3-Daren-worried, v_current_scene = Ch3-Daren-worried, v_ch3_daren_worry = 1)
choice(""Oh? Are you suggesting he can defend himself while asleep?"", Ch3-Daren-worried, v_current_scene = Ch3-Daren-worried, v_ch3_daren_worry = 2)
choice(""Damn you! I'm not leaving my friend behind!"", Ch3-Daren-worried, v_current_scene = Ch3-Daren-worried, v_ch3_daren_worry = 3)


ID: Ch3-Daren-worried
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_daren_worry == 1) {
"Why not?"

"See for yourself. Turn around and try walking back to the cave."

I hesitate, then pivot on my heel and take several strides back along our trail. Almost immediately, a strange, overwhelming impulse washes through my mind—a certainty that I need to turn left, that the path ahead is wrong, that my destination lies in the opposite direction. I stop, force myself to turn toward the cave again, and take five paces. Within seconds, I find myself veering away without realizing I altered my course.

"What is this?" I demand, staring at the trees.

"Suggestion magic," Kate says, watching me with mild satisfaction. "You won't find it in western grimoires; it is almost entirely native to Varathia. It plants an impulse in the target's mind and makes them believe the thought was their own. You noticed because you were deliberately trying to retrace your steps. A roaming mage would simply drift around the valley without ever suspecting a ward."

"How do I break through it?"

"Fix the destination in your mind. Don't think about general directions. Visualize the cave itself—the stone arch, the damp moss, the embers. As long as you hold an exact mental anchor, the suggestion slips off. I survived banshee hunters for years using this ward. It covers a wide perimeter, but it drains my reserves. I can cast it twice a day at most, and it fades after several hours. Now come. We are nearly there."

Five minutes deeper into the woods, the forest opens into a wide scar of broken timber, splintered trunks, and gouged earth—identical to yesterday's battleground, except no bodies remain. Residual magical energy hums in the air, dense enough to make the hair on my arms stand up.

"This is the place," Kate says quietly. "Tell me what you sense in the residue."

I close my eyes, reaching out with my senses to read the lingering vibrations. The weave carries the sharp, violent texture of elemental magic.

"An elementalist fought here," I tell her.

"Can you isolate the element?"

I drill deeper into the magical signatures lingering in the ozone-heavy air.

"It doesn't match the four prime elements. No scorch of fire, no chill of frost, no earth density. It carries a crackling pulse... electric in nature."

Kate's eyes widen. A faint, genuine smile touches the corners of her mouth—the first time I have seen warmth on her face since we met.

"Thank you, stillw— Barry. You have no idea how much this helps."

I blink at her, caught off guard.

"Well. Look at that. You can actually be nice."

Her smile vanishes instantly into a glare. "What is that supposed to mean?"

}
#if(v_ch3_daren_worry == 2) {
"Oh? Are you suggesting he can defend himself while asleep?"

Kate scowls at me.

"If you are so frantic about your precious paladin, walk back and check on him. I will wait here."

I turn on my heel and march back along our trail. Almost immediately, a strange, overwhelming impulse washes through my mind—a certainty that I need to turn left, that the path ahead is wrong, that my destination lies in the opposite direction. I stop, force myself to turn toward the cave again, and take five paces. Within seconds, I find myself veering away without realizing I altered my course.

"What did you do?" I demand.

"If you had asked politely instead of snapping, I might have explained," Kate says coolly. "You can live with the mystery. As you can see, no one is stumbling onto that cave tonight. Move. Our destination is just ahead."

Five minutes deeper into the woods, the forest opens into a wide scar of broken timber, splintered trunks, and gouged earth—identical to yesterday's battleground, except no bodies remain. Residual magical energy hums in the air, dense enough to make the hair on my arms stand up.

"This is the place," Kate says quietly. "Tell me what you sense in the residue."

I close my eyes, reaching out with my senses to read the lingering vibrations. The weave carries the sharp, violent texture of elemental magic.

"An elementalist fought here," I tell her.

"Can you isolate the element?"

I drill deeper into the magical signatures lingering in the ozone-heavy air.

"It doesn't match the four prime elements. No scorch of fire, no chill of frost, no earth density. It carries a crackling pulse... electric in nature."

Kate's eyes widen. A faint, genuine smile touches the corners of her mouth—the first time I have seen warmth on her face since we met.

"Thank you, stillw— Barry. You have no idea how much this helps."

I blink at her, caught off guard.

"Well. Look at that. You can actually be nice."

Her smile vanishes instantly into a glare. "What is that supposed to mean?"

}
#if(v_ch3_daren_worry == 3) {
"Damn you! I'm not leaving my friend behind!"

"When did I suggest abandoning him?" Kate snaps. "If you are so frantic about your precious paladin, walk back and check on him. I will wait here."

I turn on my heel and march back along our trail. Almost immediately, a strange, overwhelming impulse washes through my mind—a certainty that I need to turn left, that the path ahead is wrong, that my destination lies in the opposite direction. I stop, force myself to turn toward the cave again, and take five paces. Within seconds, I find myself veering away without realizing I altered my course.

