"It doesn't match the four prime elements. No scorch of fire, no chill of frost, no earth density. It carries a crackling pulse... electric in nature."

Kate's eyes widen. A faint, genuine smile touches the corners of her mouth—the first time I have seen warmth on her face since we met.

"Thank you, stillw— Barry. You have no idea how much this helps."

I blink at her, caught off guard.

"Well. Look at that. You can actually be nice."

Her smile vanishes instantly into a glare. "What is that supposed to mean?"

}
choice("Continue", Ch3-Dart, v_current_scene = Ch3-Dart)


ID: Ch3-Dart
TEXT:

set(v_ac_ch3_close,1) if (v_perception == 3 && v_hearing >= 2 || v_perception == 3 && v_premonition >= 2)
set(v_ch3_saved_from_dart,1) if (v_perception == 3 && v_hearing >= 2 || v_perception == 3 && v_premonition >= 2)
set(v_ch3_saved_from_dart,0) if (v_hearing < 2 && v_premonition < 2 || v_perception < 3)
achievement("That was close!",v_ac_ch3_close)
#if(v_perception == 3 && v_premonition >= 2) {

A violent jolt of foresight hits my skull—a vision of a steel-tipped dart slicing out of the darkness straight toward Kate's throat.

I spin on my heel, scanning the perimeter. Thirty yards back, half-hidden behind a thick pine trunk, two figures crouch in the shadows. One of them levels a heavy steel crossbow directly at Kate.

"Barry?" Kate asks, stepping toward me. "What do you—"

The trigger snaps. I lunge, driving my shoulder into Kate's ribs and shoving her hard to the dirt. The dart hisses through the exact space where her neck was a fraction of a second ago, burying itself deep into a fallen log with a dull thud.

"You blundering fool!" a voice shrieks from the tree line—high-pitched, reedy, and shrill enough to belong to a woman. "How do you miss a stationary target? Plan B! Deploy the smoke! Now!"

Cylinders hiss in the brush, vomiting thick, pungent alchemical smoke that swallows the clearing in seconds. Between the darkness and the grey fog, the trees vanish into murky silhouettes, giving off only the faint, scattered warmth of mortal auras.

"Surrender, banshee!" the shrill voice screeches through the haze. "You are surrounded! You cannot see through this shroud. Lay down your arms, and we will grant you and your human pet a swift, painless throat-slit!"

Another hunter convinced I am a stillwater's lackey. The bluff is wearing thin.

"Is this a joke?" Kate's voice rings cold and contemptuous across the clearing. "I count at least a hundred men in the brush, and not a single spark of magic among you. Do you truly believe numbers alone can kill a mage?"

"Mages bleed like pigs," the voice snaps back. "And if you bleed, you die!"

A hunting horn bellows, a deafening blast that drowns out the forest, followed by the simultaneous thrum of dozens of bowstrings. Crossbow bolts rain down through the smoke. Kate raises both palms, and a heavy dome of crystalline blue ice erupts from the frozen earth, enclosing us completely. Bolts shatter and ricochet off the frost with deafening cracks.

"Barry," Kate breathes, holding the spell steady. "Can you pinpoint them through the smoke?"

I press my hand against the semi-translucent ice wall. My heightened vision cuts through the grey fog like a blade, mapping every crossbowman reloading behind the timber.

"Clear as glass."

"Good. You are my eyes. When I drop the dome, call their marks. I will wipe them out while keeping you covered."

}
#if(v_premonition >= 2 && v_perception < 3) {

A violent jolt of foresight hits my skull—a vision of a steel-tipped dart slicing out of the darkness straight toward Kate's throat.

I wheel around, searching the dense shadows, but the night is pitch black and the trees blur together in a wall of pine and brush.

"Barry?" Kate asks, turning toward me. "What do you—"

A sharp hiss cuts the night. Before I can move, an iron dart thuds into Kate's left shoulder. She drops to one knee with a sharp gasp, clutching the puncture.

"I'm... fine," she whispers through gritted teeth, though her skin drains of color and her eyelids flutter as she fights to stay upright.

A shrill, high-pitched laugh rings out from the trees.

"As expected of a banshee! Even concentrated wyvern venom barely drops you. But you are on your knees, monster. Surrender now, and we will grant you and your human pet a swift, merciful end."

Another hunter convinced I am a stillwater's lackey. The bluff is wearing thin.

"Is this a joke?" Kate forces the words out, her voice raw and slurred with exhaustion. "A hundred men in the brush... not a single caster... you think numbers can kill a mage?"

"Mages bleed like pigs," the voice snaps back. "And if you bleed, you die!"

A hunting horn bellows, a deafening blast that drowns out the forest, followed by the simultaneous thrum of dozens of bowstrings. Crossbow bolts rain down through the darkness. Kate slams her palms into the soil, forcing a thick dome of crystalline ice into existence around us. Bolts shatter against the frost wall in a furious spray of iron and splinters.

"Barry," Kate gasps, sweat dripping from her jaw as her left eye twitches violently. "Listen to me. I cannot hold this barrier long. Run back to the cave and fetch Daren. We cannot survive this alone."

"You can't hold out here!" I say. "You're barely conscious!"

"I will hold. I have survived worse than poison and mercenaries. Go, before they flank the perimeter!"

}
#if(v_perception == 3 && v_hearing >= 2 && v_premonition < 2) {

A muffled hiss drifts from the brush behind us: "What are you waiting for? Shoot her!"

I spin around instantly, tracking the sound. Thirty yards back, half-hidden behind a thick pine trunk, two figures crouch in the shadows. One of them levels a heavy steel crossbow directly at Kate.

