choice("Go with her plan", Ch3-Plan, v_current_scene = Ch3-Plan) if (v_perception == 3 && v_hearing >= 2 || v_perception == 3 && v_premonition >= 2)
choice("Tell her I'd rather fight the enemies head on and ask for an ice dagger", Ch3-Saved-Dagger, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Dagger) if (v_perception == 3 && v_hearing >= 2 || v_perception == 3 && v_premonition >= 2)
choice("Go and call for Daren", Ch3-Call-Daren, v_current_scene = Ch3-Call-Daren, v_ch3_treasure = 0) if (v_hearing < 2 && v_premonition < 2 || v_perception < 3)
choice("Ask Kate for an ice dagger, and deal with the attackers myself", Ch3-Give, v_current_scene = Ch3-Give) if (v_hearing < 2 && v_premonition < 2 || v_perception < 3)


ID: Ch3-Call-Daren
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_ch3_daren_worry != 1)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_ch3_daren_worry == 1)
#if(v_ch3_daren_worry != 1) {
Taking on a hundred armed mercenaries alone is suicide. I nod once.

"Hold the line," I tell Kate, stepping toward the gap in the frost. "Stay alive until I return."

As I slip into the dark pines, Kate calls out behind me:

"Wait! The ward—"

The war horn screams, drowning her words as she seals the ice dome against another rain of bolts. There is no time to double back.

I sprint between the trees, ducking beneath branches and bypassing two mercenaries focused on the ice dome. I tear through the woods toward the mountain ridge.

As I close in on our valley, a heavy fog settles over my thoughts. An urgent compulsion pulls me left, telling me the cave lies the other way. I turn, take twenty paces, realize I am heading back downhill, and try to retrace my steps. Every attempt veers off course.

Kate's suggestion ward. In the rush of battle, I forgot her barrier entirely.

I stumble in circles through the dark brush, my heart pounding in panic. A dry branch snaps behind me. Before I can draw my dagger, an iron bolt punches through my shoulder blade, driving me to my knees. Two more bolts slam into my chest. Blood fills my throat, and the dark forest tilts into blackness.

}
#if(v_ch3_daren_worry == 1) {
Taking on a hundred armed mercenaries alone is suicide. I nod once.

"Hold the line," I tell Kate, stepping toward the gap in the frost. "Stay alive until I return."

I slip into the dark pines, sprinting past two skirmishers whose eyes are glued to the glowing ice dome. I keep my head down, boots tearing through moss and gravel as I race toward the mountain ridge.

As I approach the perimeter of Kate's ward, the familiar compulsion tugs at my thoughts. I close my eyes for a heartbeat, visualizing the damp stone archway of our cavern, the smell of lichen, the dying campfire. The fog clears instantly. I push through the barrier and sprint up the final slope into the cave.

I drop beside Daren's bedroll, grabbing his tunic and shaking him hard.

"Daren! Up! Kate is ambushed. Move!"

Daren bolts upright, his eyes instantly sharp. "How far?"

"A mile south in the pines. An entire battalion."

He doesn't waste another second on questions, hauling his plate over his shoulders and buckling his scabbard as we charge down the mountain.

"Good gods, Barry, how did the two of you wander into an army?"

We hit the forest at a dead run. As we near the clearing, the war horns have ceased. The woods are dead silent. Bodies litter the brush in tangled heaps, frost riming their armor and faces.

In the center of the clearing, surrounded by scores of corpses, Kate kneels in the crimson-stained needles. Both of her hands rest on the pommel of an ice broadsword impaled in the earth. Blood coats her tunic and sleeves. As our boots crunch on the gravel, she raises her head, her face pale as snow.

"I apologize," she whispers, a faint, bitter edge in her voice. "I should have left a few for you."

Her grip slips from the hilt, and she collapses forward into the dirt.

"Kate!" Daren drops his shield and dives to her side, rolling her over. White light flares from his palms as he presses them against her chest, but his breathing is ragged and the glow flickers. Several deep puncture wounds across her torso refuse to close.

"The trauma is too severe for field healing," Daren says, his jaw set in grim fury. "Her mana core is drained dry. She will survive, but she needs days of absolute rest. Now explain to me why the two of you were wandering this far from camp in the middle of the night."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_ch3_daren_worry != 1)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_ch3_daren_worry != 1)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_ch3_daren_worry != 1)
choice(""She wanted to ask me in private why we didn't try to purify her."", Ch3-Private, v_current_scene = Ch3-Private) if (v_ch3_daren_worry == 1)
choice(""We thought we could handle it."", Ch3-Handle, v_current_scene = Ch3-Handle) if (v_ch3_daren_worry == 1)
choice(""Kate cast a barrier that prevents enemies from approaching our cave."", Ch3-Barrier, v_current_scene = Ch3-Barrier) if (v_ch3_daren_worry == 1)


ID: Ch3-Private
TEXT:

"She wanted to ask me in private why we didn't try to purify her. She was afraid that if she'd ask you, personally, she might give you ideas."

Daren stares down at her bloodied face, a muscle working in his cheek.

"So she knows she is destined to awaken as a banshee. Does she not realize white-magic purification carries almost no risk when performed early?"

choice(""She never said that she's a banshee. Just that the evidence pointed that way."", Ch3-Evidence, v_current_scene = Ch3-Evidence)
choice(""Actually she was born human, but became a mage afterwards."", Ch3-Mage, v_current_scene = Ch3-Mage)


ID: Ch3-Handle
TEXT:

"We thought we could handle it by ourselves. It wasn't that far away and she needed my help to study some magical traces."

"You thought wrong," Daren says grimly, gathering Kate into his arms and lifting her off the bloodstained ground. "Let us move before lingering scouts bring reinforcements down on this clearing."

He carries her back through the dark woods to our cave, laying her gently near the dead fire on a thick bed of blankets to rest.

choice("Next chapter", Ch4-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch3-Barrier
TEXT:

"Kate needed my help to study some magical traces, and this place wasn't that far away. She cast a barrier that prevents enemies from approaching our cave, so you'd be safe while we were gone."

"I am not concerned with my own hide, Barry! I am concerned with yours!" Daren snaps, hauling Kate into his arms. "This is why parties hold formation. The moment we divide, disaster follows. Come. We move before enemy scouts regroup."

He carries her back through the dark woods to our cave, laying her gently near the dead fire on a thick bed of blankets to rest.

choice("Next chapter", Ch4-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch3-Evidence
TEXT:

"She never said that she's a banshee. Just that the evidence pointed that way."

"You cannot look away from reality forever, Barry," Daren says quietly, gathering Kate into his arms. "I pray that when your eyes finally open, it won't be too late to save her. Come. We move before scouts return."

He carries her back through the dark woods to our cave, laying her gently near the dead fire on a thick bed of blankets to rest.

choice("Next chapter", Ch4-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch3-Mage
TEXT:

"Actually she was born human, but became a mage afterwards."

Daren stops, looking at me with heavy skepticism.

"She told you this, and you took it as truth? Women are not mages, Barry. Denying facts will only bring tragedy. Come. We need to leave before lingering scouts bring more men."

He gathers Kate into his arms, carrying her back through the dark woods to our cave, and lays her gently near the dead fire on a thick bed of blankets to rest.

choice("Next chapter", Ch4-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch3-Plan
TEXT:

I give the nod, and Kate drops the front arc of her dome.

"Low behind that fallen pine, thirty paces right," I call out.

She flicks her wrist. A jagged cone of ice tears through the smoke, catching the mercenary square in the chest with a wet crunch.

"Down! Next one by the split birch."

"Which side?"

"Left flank."

"Height?"

"Five foot eight."

A second frost spike shrieks through the fog, punching through the man's temple.

"Target down. Next one is—"

"Too slow," Kate cuts in, weaving floating ice shields to deflect a volley of incoming bolts. "I have a faster method."

She raises both arms, summoning two massive, rotating fans of solid ice ten feet above the smoke line. They spin with a deafening gale, driving howling downdrafts that tear the chemical fog to ribbons in seconds.

"It's clearing," she says, raising three more frost turbines.

"No!" the shrill voice shrieks from the trees. "Shatter the fans! Shoot her down!"

Too late. The moonlight floods the stripped clearing, exposing dozens of crossbowmen caught in the open. Above Kate's head, hundreds of razor-sharp ice daggers coalesce from the freezing air. With a single downward sweep of her arms, she releases the storm.

A shrieking wave of frost scythes through the woods. The crossbowmen never even raised their weapons; the volley shredded them where they stood, pinning bodies against trunks and ripping through leather armor.

A lone survivor leaps from behind a rock on the left, lunging in a desperate flanking charge. Kate doesn't even glance his way; a single ice shard whips from her fingertips and takes him through the forehead. His body crashes into the gravel, his repeating crossbow tumbling across the dirt and coming to rest at my boots.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Ice-hand, v_current_scene = Ch3-Ice-hand)


ID: Ch3-Ice-hand
TEXT:

I scoop up the repeating crossbow and check the magazine. Behind me, a massive sculpted arm of solid ice extends into the dark woods, dragging a man through the dirt by the collar of his embroidered velvet tunic. His fancy silk doublet and gold-threaded cape belong in a western palace, not a muddy forest.

"Unhand me!" he screams in that shrill, reedy voice, thrashing helplessly in the giant frost grip. "In the name of the Duke of Bagelberry, release me this instant!"

The ice hand ignores him, hauling him forward and dumping him into the dirt at our feet. He catches sight of Kate's cold glare and lets out a terrified squeak, scrambling backward until he hits a tree root and sprawls in the mud.

"Mercy!" he wails, tears cutting streaks through the grime on his cheeks.

He throws himself flat against the ground, trembling violently.

"I only entered the tournament for prestige! I never meant to harm you! Spare me! I have wealth—endless wealth! Take it! Take all of it!"

He fumbles a heavy velvet pouch from his sash and hurls it onto the needles. Gold coins spill across the moss. There is enough here to buy an estate in the Western Continent and live in luxury for decades.

choice("Take his money and spare his life", Ch3-Saved-Spare-Take, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Spare-Take, v_ch3_treasure = 1)
choice("Spare his life but don't take his money", Ch3-Saved-Spare-Leave, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Spare-Leave, v_ch3_treasure = 0)
choice("Kill him, and take his money", Ch3-Saved-Kill-Take, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Kill-Take, v_ch3_treasure = 1)
choice("Kill him, but don't take his money", Ch3-Saved-Kill-Leave, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Kill-Leave, v_ch3_treasure = 0)
choice("Pressure him into telling me where he's hiding the rest of his gold", Ch3-Saved-Pressure, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Pressure, v_ch3_treasure = 2)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Spare-Take
TEXT:

Looking down at the sniveling wreck, I doubt he carries enough spine to ever cross our path again.

"Take your life," I say, picking up the pouch of gold. "Get out of our sight and don't look back."

"Yes! Bless you! You will never see me again, I swear on my ancestors!"

He scrambles onto his hands and knees, bolting toward the dark pines.

He doesn't make it two paces. A thick spire of black-veined ice erupts from the earth beneath his chest, lifting him off his feet with a sickening tear of flesh and bone.

"Why..." he chokes, blood foaming at his lips as his eyes roll back. His body goes limp on the spire.

"Kate!" I stare at the impaled corpse, nausea tightening my throat. "What did you do? He surrendered!"

"He did," Kate says, her voice cold and level. "And I granted him a swift end. Exactly what he promised us five minutes ago."

"He was defenseless. That was murder."

Kate turns to me, her dark eyes entirely devoid of remorse.

"Listen to me, Barry. I didn't kill him for petty spite. That man commanded enough gold and influence to march a hundred mercenaries to their deaths on a whim. The moment his panic subsided, he would have bought a fresh battalion and made hunting us his personal crusade. People with that much pride and coin never let a humiliation stand. We have enough killers on our trail without leaving a vindictive noble alive to finance our executions."

I look from her to the corpse, the grim calculus settling like lead in my chest.

"I... hadn't considered that."

"Of course you hadn't. Pick up the gold. We are leaving."

I pocket the purse and follow her into the dark woods.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Saved-Return, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Return)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Spare-Leave
TEXT:

Looking down at the sniveling wreck, I doubt he carries enough spine to ever cross our path again.

"Keep your coin," I say, nudging the bag aside. "Get out of our sight and don't look back."

"Yes! Bless you! You will never see me again, I swear on my ancestors!"

He grabs his purse and scrambles onto his hands and knees, bolting toward the dark pines.

He doesn't make it two paces. A thick spire of black-veined ice erupts from the earth beneath his chest, lifting him off his feet with a sickening tear of flesh and bone.

"Why..." he chokes, blood foaming at his lips as his eyes roll back. His body goes limp on the spire.

"Kate!" I stare at the impaled corpse, nausea tightening my throat. "What did you do? He surrendered!"

"He did," Kate says, her voice cold and level. "And I granted him a swift end. Exactly what he promised us five minutes ago."

"He was defenseless. That was murder."

Kate turns to me, her dark eyes entirely devoid of remorse.

"Listen to me, Barry. I didn't kill him for petty spite. That man commanded enough gold and influence to march a hundred mercenaries to their deaths on a whim. The moment his panic subsided, he would have bought a fresh battalion and made hunting us his personal crusade. People with that much pride and coin never let a humiliation stand. We have enough killers on our trail without leaving a vindictive noble alive to finance our executions."

I look from her to the corpse, the grim calculus settling like lead in my chest.

"I... hadn't considered that."

"Of course you hadn't. We are leaving."

I turn away from the body and follow her into the dark woods.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Saved-Return, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Return)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Kill-Take
TEXT:

I raise the repeating crossbow and put a bolt through his forehead before he can draw another breath. In his place, he would have done the same to us without a second thought.

I pick up the heavy pouch of gold and sling it over my belt.

"Done," I tell Kate. "Let's get back to the cave."

choice("Continue", Ch3-Saved-Return, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Return)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Kill-Leave
TEXT:

I raise the repeating crossbow and put a bolt through his forehead before he can draw another breath. In his place, he would have done the same to us without a second thought.

I leave the bag of gold in the mud. Dead weight on the march, and useless in a magical tournament.

"Done," I tell Kate. "Let's get back to the cave."

choice("Continue", Ch3-Saved-Return, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Return)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Pressure
TEXT:

"You claim this is all your wealth," I say, tapping the crossbow against my palm. "A noble doesn't travel without an emergency reserve. Where is the rest of it?"

"What do you mean?" he stammers, sweating profusely. "That is everything I carried! The remainder of my estate is in the West!"

"I know how warlords think. You wouldn't keep your entire purse in one satchel on an ambush. Where is the cache?"

"I swear by the gods, that is all I have!"

"Unfortunate."

I level the iron sights directly between his eyes and slowly pull the trigger back.

"Wait! Wait! By the roots of the hollow cedar!" he shrieks, pointing frantically toward a gnarled pine forty feet back. "Buried under the stone! That is the last of it, I swear on my life!"

I walk to the hollow tree and dig through the loose needles, unearthing a heavy iron-bound strongbox packed with gold bullion and gemstones. It holds three times the value of his belt pouch.

I lock the box and return to the clearing.

"You see?" the duke whimpers from the dirt. "It is all yours! Every coin!"

choice("Spare his life", Ch3-Saved-Pressure-Spare, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Pressure-Spare)
choice("Kill him anyway", Ch3-Saved-Pressure-Kill, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Pressure-Kill)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Pressure-Spare
TEXT:

"Take your life," I say, hauling the strongbox up. "Get out of our sight and never return."

"Yes! Bless you! Never again, I swear it!"

He scrambles to his feet and sprints toward the tree line.

He doesn't make it two paces. A thick spire of black-veined ice erupts from the earth beneath his chest, lifting him off his feet with a sickening tear of flesh and bone.

"Why..." he chokes, blood foaming at his lips as his eyes roll back. His body goes limp on the spire.

"Kate!" I stare at the impaled corpse, nausea tightening my throat. "What did you do? He surrendered!"

"He did," Kate says, her voice cold and level. "And I granted him a swift end. Exactly what he promised us five minutes ago."

"He was defenseless. That was murder."

Kate turns to me, her dark eyes entirely devoid of remorse.

"Listen to me, Barry. I didn't kill him for petty spite. That man commanded enough gold and influence to march a hundred mercenaries to their deaths on a whim. The moment his panic subsided, he would have bought a fresh battalion and made hunting us his personal crusade. People with that much pride and coin never let a humiliation stand. We have enough killers on our trail without leaving a vindictive noble alive to finance our executions."

I look from her to the corpse, the grim calculus settling like lead in my chest.

"I... hadn't considered that."

"Of course you hadn't. Grab the gold. We are leaving."

I shoulder the treasure and follow her into the dark woods.

choice("Continue", Ch3-Saved-Return, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Return)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Pressure-Kill
TEXT:

"Am... am I free to go?" the duke stammers, looking up from the mud.

"You are free," I say, centering the iron sights on his temple. "Forever."

The bowstring snaps. The bolt punches clean through his skull, dropping him instantly into the pine needles.

I gather the strongbox and the velvet pouch, turning to Kate.

"Done. Let's head back."

choice("Continue", Ch3-Saved-Return, v_current_scene = Ch3-Saved-Return)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Return
TEXT:

#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 1) {
We trek back through the dark forest in silence. As the mountain ridge looms ahead, Kate slows her pace, glancing over her shoulder at me.

"That dart was laced with wyvern venom," she says quietly. "If you hadn't shoved me clear, I would be dead or chained in an iron wagon by sunrise."

She pauses, her expression unreadable in the moonlight.

"I owe you a life, Barry. I don't forget my debts."

We walk the rest of the trail without another word. The silence is no longer cold; after a dawn spent knee-deep in blood, the quiet is a relief.

}
#if(v_ch2_kate_debt == 0) {
We trek back through the dark forest in silence. As the mountain ridge looms ahead, Kate slows her pace, glancing over her shoulder at me.

"That dart was laced with wyvern venom," she says quietly. "If you hadn't shoved me clear, I would be dead or chained in an iron wagon by sunrise."

She pauses, a faint, wry quirk touching her lip.

"That makes two debts I owe you, Barry. Have you considered opening a bank?"

"I'll look into a charter after we survive the tournament."

We walk the rest of the trail without another word. The silence is no longer cold; after a dawn spent knee-deep in blood, the quiet is a relief.

}
choice("Next chapter", Ch4-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = Ch4-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt = 0, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch3-Saved-Dagger
TEXT:

"Actually, I'd rather take the fight directly to them. Can you forge an ice dagger for me?"

"Are you out of your mind?" Kate stares at me.

She studies my face for a moment, realizes I am serious, and lets out a sharp breath.

"Fine. Die your own way."

She weaves frost between her palms, shaping a twelve-inch stiletto of dense, translucent blue ice, and presses it into my hand. The hilt burns with biting cold, but the grip is grooved and balanced. This is no time to complain about frostbite.

She parts a seam in the ice dome. I duck through the narrow opening and sprint headlong into the smoking forest.

Thirty yards deep in the pines, I spot a mercenary crouching behind a wide oak trunk, his back turned as he cranks the lever on his repeating crossbow. He hasn't heard a sound.

choice("Kill him with my dagger", Ch3-First-kill, v_current_scene = Ch3-First-kill, v_ch3_first_killed = 1)
choice("Use the hilt of the dagger to knock him out", Ch3-First-Knock, v_current_scene = Ch3-First-Knock)


ID: Ch3-First-kill
TEXT:

I close the distance through the shadows, step behind the pine trunk, and hook my left arm around his jaw, dragging his head back as the ice dagger saws across his throat.

Hot arterial blood coats my fingers. The man thrashes, clutching his severed windpipe, but only a wet, drowning gurgle escapes his lips before his knees buckle and he slumps into the needles.

This is the first time I have taken a human life. It was...

choice("Easy", Ch3-First-Easy, v_current_scene = Ch3-First-Easy)
choice("Necessary", Ch3-First-Necessary, v_current_scene = Ch3-First-Necessary)
choice("Horrifying", Ch3-First-Horrifying, v_current_scene = Ch3-First-Horrifying)


ID: Ch3-First-Easy
TEXT:

...easy. Terrifyingly easy. No paralysis, no hesitation. My hands moved on instinct, executing the kill as cleanly as gutting fish. A grim relief settles over me—I will have to cut down many more before the Magium is within reach, and my resolve will not break.

The wet gurgle of the dying man echoes through the immediate trees.

Heavy footsteps pound through the brush. Five mercenaries peel away from the firing line on Kate's dome, crossbows raised as they rush my position.

I scan the ground. The dead man's weapon is a repeating crossbow, its magazine slotted with five steel bolts. If my aim holds, every bolt has a mark.

choice("Hide behind a tree, hoping they will leave on their own", Ch3-Hide, v_current_scene = Ch3-Hide)
choice("Use the crossbow, but try not to kill them", Ch3-Second-Alive, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Alive)
choice("Use the crossbow, and aim for the kill", Ch3-Second-Kill, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Kill, v_ch3_second_killed = 1)


ID: Ch3-First-Necessary
TEXT:

...necessary. No thrill, no satisfaction, but no guilt either. In this tournament, hesitation is a death sentence. He would have driven a bolt through my skull without a second thought. We entered these woods knowing the price of failure.

The wet gurgle of the dying man echoes through the immediate trees.

Heavy footsteps pound through the brush. Five mercenaries peel away from the firing line on Kate's dome, crossbows raised as they rush my position.

I scan the ground. The dead man's weapon is a repeating crossbow, its magazine slotted with five steel bolts. If my aim holds, every bolt has a mark.

choice("Hide behind a tree, hoping they will leave on their own", Ch3-Hide, v_current_scene = Ch3-Hide)
choice("Use the crossbow, but try not to kill them", Ch3-Second-Alive, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Alive)
choice("Use the crossbow, and aim for the kill", Ch3-Second-Kill, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Kill, v_ch3_second_killed = 1)


ID: Ch3-First-Horrifying
TEXT:

...horrifying. Hot blood drenches my sleeves, sticky and metallic. The sickening reality of extinguishing a human life hits my gut like a punch. Somewhere across the ocean, a family will wait for a man who is rotting in a Varathian forest. I force the tremor from my hands—I cannot afford remorse while Kate is pinned down.

The wet gurgle of the dying man echoes through the immediate trees.

Heavy footsteps pound through the brush. Five mercenaries peel away from the firing line on Kate's dome, crossbows raised as they rush my position.

I scan the ground. The dead man's weapon is a repeating crossbow, its magazine slotted with five steel bolts. If my aim holds, every bolt has a mark.

choice("Hide behind a tree, hoping they will leave on their own", Ch3-Hide, v_current_scene = Ch3-Hide)
choice("Use the crossbow, but try not to kill them", Ch3-Second-Alive, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Alive)
choice("Use the crossbow, and aim for the kill", Ch3-Second-Kill, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Kill, v_ch3_second_killed = 1)


ID: Ch3-First-Knock
TEXT:

I close the distance through the shadows, step behind the pine trunk, and smash the weighted frost pommel of the dagger into the base of his skull.

Bone cracks dull under the blow. The man lets out a choked shout before collapsing limp into the needles, his cry carrying through the trees.

Heavy footsteps pound through the brush. Five mercenaries peel away from the firing line on Kate's dome, crossbows raised as they rush my position.

I scan the ground. The unconscious man's weapon is a repeating crossbow, its magazine slotted with five steel bolts. If my aim holds, every bolt has a mark.

choice("Hide behind a tree, hoping they will leave on their own", Ch3-Hide, v_current_scene = Ch3-Hide)
choice("Use the crossbow, but try not to kill them", Ch3-Second-Alive, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Alive)
choice("Use the crossbow, and aim for the kill", Ch3-Second-Kill, v_current_scene = Ch3-Second-Kill, v_ch3_second_killed = 1)


ID: Ch3-Hide
TEXT:

I back away into the deep underbrush, pressing my spine flat against the trunk of a broad cedar.

Seconds later, boots crunch over dry pine needles as the five mercenaries surround their fallen comrade.

"Who did this? Show yourself, coward!" one barks.

"The banshee? Could she strike through the wood?"

"Don't be an idiot. The banshee is trapped under our fire."

While they argue over the body, I prepare to slip deeper into the shadows. A leather glove clamps over my mouth from behind the cedar trunk. A steel blade bites across my throat, severing muscle and artery in a single vicious pull.

"Caught like a rat," a voice grunts as my legs give out.

The mercenary steps over me, raising his blade for a downward thrust as darkness closes in.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: Ch3-Second-Alive
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_combat_technique >= 2 || v_reflexes >= 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_combat_technique < 2 && v_reflexes < 2)
#if(v_combat_technique >= 2) {

I scoop up the repeating crossbow and step out from behind the trunk, leveling the iron sights on the lead mercenary.

Combat theory guides my trigger finger: I aim for the clavicle junction, disabling the shoulder without puncturing the pleural cavity. The bolt strikes true, dropping him with a howl. I pivot, putting a second bolt through his partner's bicep before diving behind a nearby pine as three return bolts shred the bark where I stood.

Three shots left in the cylinder.

I roll out from the trunk. Instead of taking cover, the three remaining mercenaries have formed a defensive wall over their wounded allies. Perfect. Out in the open, their center mass is completely exposed.

I loose two rapid shots, piercing their thighs. Both collapse into the mud with agonized groans.

The last man standing levels his crossbow, but I duck beneath his line of fire, rising to put my final bolt through his knee.

He crashes to the ground, screaming. I melt into the dark woods before the noise draws the rest of the company. These five won't be chasing anyone tonight.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 2 && v_combat_technique < 2) {

I scoop up the repeating crossbow and step out from behind the trunk, leveling the sights on the center man.

Avoiding lethal torso hits, I aim low, driving a bolt through his right thigh. I pivot, catching a second man in the shin, and immediately duck behind a tree as three return bolts hiss through the air where my head was.

Three shots left in the cylinder.

I pivot out from the trunk. The three uninjured mercenaries have clustered in front of their fallen comrades to shield them.

I fire twice into their legs, dropping both with dull thuds against the damp earth.

The last man raises his weapon, but I slip his shot with a quick roll.

A sudden scrape of metal on stone catches my ear. One of the wounded men on the ground has recovered his crossbow, aiming straight at my ribs from point-blank range.

Time stretches thin. The bolt leaves the channel in slow motion, glinting in the dark. Reflexes fire automatically—I jerk my torso left, the iron tip slicing clean through my tunic and nicking my ribs. I spin, locking eyes with the last standing mercenary, and loose my final bolt into his hip.

He collapses beside his comrades, writhing in pain. I disappear into the deep pines before the yelling draws reinforcements.

}
#if(v_combat_technique < 2 && v_reflexes < 2) {

I scoop up the repeating crossbow and step out from behind the trunk, leveling the sights on the center man.

Avoiding lethal torso hits, I aim low, driving a bolt through his right thigh. I pivot, catching a second man in the shin, and immediately duck behind a tree as three return bolts hiss through the air where my head was.

Three shots left in the cylinder.

I step out, firing twice into the legs of the standing men, dropping both to the dirt.

The last man raises his weapon, but I duck his shot.

I fail to notice the first mercenary dragging himself up on one elbow, his repeating crossbow leveled directly at my face.

There is no time to move. The bolt punches straight between my eyes, killing me instantly.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_combat_technique < 2 && v_reflexes < 2)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_combat_technique < 2 && v_reflexes < 2)
